{
  "teamId": 137,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 San Francisco Giants entered the season with cautious optimism, bolstered by the arrivals of Andrew McCutchen and Evan Longoria, yet the roster was aging and the window for contention was narrowing. April was a study in volatility — McCutchen's early three-run blast signaled promise, but Hunter Strickland's chronic inability to protect leads quickly became a recurring nightmare. The bullpen, so often the Giants' backbone during their championship years, was crumbling, and a 13-13 April record masked just how fragile the foundation truly was.\n\nMay brought little relief, with a 11-16 skid that exposed the team's offensive limitations — a league-worst OPS of .668 that would persist all year. The pitching staff held its own with a respectable 3.95 ERA, but runs were impossible to manufacture. Then, surprisingly, June ignited. An 18-10 month, the best of the season, was fueled by unlikely heroes: Alen Hanson delivering a go-ahead homer, Brandon Crawford coming up clutch, Hunter Pence looking revitalized. For a fleeting moment in midsummer, the Giants dared to dream of a wild card.\n\nJuly and August told a crueler story. The bullpen — Will Smith, Sam Dyson, Ray Black — kept surrendering critical leads at the worst possible moments. A disastrous three-game stretch against the Astros in early August, punctuated by a Marwin Gonzalez three-run blast off Will Smith, effectively buried any lingering playoff hopes. The team went 11-14 in July and 13-14 in August, treading water while the rest of the NL West pulled away.\n\nSeptember was a collapse of historic proportions — a 5-21 record that turned a disappointing season into an outright disaster. Madison Bumgarner surrendering a grand slam, Mark Melancon blowing yet another late lead, and even a consolation double by aging veteran Gregor Blanco in the final week summed up the season's bittersweet absurdity. The Giants finished 73-89, sixteen games under .500, signaling that the dynasty was not merely pausing — it was over.",
